Answer: Create a class and make it extend the Exception class. Just giving a class a name such as MyException does not make it behave like an exception; only when it extends Exception (or one of its subclasses) it becomes an exception class. Methods of Exception, such as getMessage(), are then available and can be overridden.

```java
class InvalidPinException extends Exception {
@Override
public String getMessage() {
return "Invalid pin number";
}
}
```

Answer: InvalidPinException extends Exception and overrides getMessage() to return the description "Invalid pin number". In verify(), if the entered pin is not equal to the customer's pin, an object of InvalidPinException is created and thrown with the throw keyword. Because it is a checked exception, verify() declares it with throws.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class InvalidPinException extends Exception {
@Override
public String getMessage() {
return "Invalid pin number";
}
}
class ATM {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
int customerPin = 1234;
int enteredPin;
```

void acceptInput() {

```java
System.out.println("Enter the pin number");
enteredPin = sc.nextInt();
}
```

void verify() throws InvalidPinException {

```java
if (enteredPin == customerPin) {
System.out.println("Collect your money");
```

} else {

```java
InvalidPinException e = new InvalidPinException();
throw e;
}
}
}
```

Answer: The bank takes the pin through the ATM and verifies it inside a try block. If the exception is caught, the bank prints the message and gives the next chance by calling acceptInput() again, with the verification again placed inside a try block, which is written inside the catch block (nested try-catch). After the third wrong pin, the bank blocks the card. Sample input: 3333, 4444, 5555.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class InvalidPinException extends Exception {
@Override
public String getMessage() {
return "Invalid pin number";
}
}
class ATM {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
int customerPin = 1234;
int enteredPin;
```

void acceptInput() {

```java
System.out.println("Enter the pin number");
enteredPin = sc.nextInt();
}
```

void verify() throws InvalidPinException {

```java
if (enteredPin == customerPin) {
System.out.println("Collect your money");
```

} else {

```java
InvalidPinException e = new InvalidPinException();
throw e;
}
}
}
class Bank {
```

void init() {

```java
ATM atm = new ATM();
atm.acceptInput();
try {
atm.verify();
```

} catch (InvalidPinException e) {

```java
System.out.println(e.getMessage());
atm.acceptInput();
try {
atm.verify();
```

} catch (InvalidPinException e2) {

```java
System.out.println(e2.getMessage());
atm.acceptInput();
try {
atm.verify();
```

} catch (InvalidPinException e3) {

```java
System.out.println(e3.getMessage());
System.out.println("Your card is blocked, please contact bank");
}
}
}
}
}
public class RBI {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Bank bank = new Bank();
bank.init();
}
}
```

Output when all three pins are wrong: Enter the pin number Invalid pin number Enter the pin number Invalid pin number Enter the pin number Invalid pin number Your card is blocked, please contact bank Output when the first pin is wrong (3333) and the second is correct (1234): Enter the pin number Invalid pin number Enter the pin number Collect your money

Answer: getMessage() is a method of the exception class that returns the description of the exception as a String. In the custom exception it is overridden to return "Invalid pin number". In the catch block, you do not print the text yourself; you call e.getMessage() with the reference e of the exception object, and it returns the description, which is then printed.

```java
catch (InvalidPinException e) {
System.out.println(e.getMessage());   // Invalid pin number
}
```
